## Chapter 1.5: Prime Suspect

It certainly was not easy to accept. If what Luka deduced was true, then he might have just exposed the perpetrator’s ultimate plan: Igor wasn’t dead, and the charred corpse everyone assumed was him belonged to someone else entirely. Could this be the work of Igor himself? Or an assailant, perhaps? But to what end? And who was the poor soul that had taken his place? A thousand more questions surged through his mind until…

“Luka… Hey… Luka!” A familiar voice cut through the haze.

Luka snapped back to reality at the sound, the vision dissolving around him. Nadya was standing right in front of him on the other side of the cordon tape, waving a gloved hand before his face.

“A-Ah. Yeah?” Luka shook his head to clear the lingering after-images.

“Used your _vision_ again, haven’t you?” she teased, still clearly skeptical of his whole shtick.

“Yeah… Sorry. What were you about to say?” Luka tried to sound polite, choosing not to burden her with his condition.

“No, no, it was nothing. I mean, I’ve seen the crime scene, and my boss already bit my head off,” she made an exaggerated sarcastic gesture. “So, I’m heading back to the station. Care to join?”

“Uh—sure! I’ve seen more than enough…” Luka was still unsure whether he should simply let this go or speak up.

At his answer, Nadya ducked under the tape to join him, leading the way through the murmuring crowd. Once they cleared the congested zone, Luka couldn’t keep the thought to himself any longer.

“Hey, Nadya,” Luka began.

“Yeah?” Nadya slowed her pace, letting him fall in beside her.

“May I ask you a favor?”

“Sure, shoot.”

“Do you have a way to look someone up?”

Nadya giggled. “Come now, abusing official authority already? What’s the—” She cut herself off mid-sentence. Having dragged Luka all the way out here, she felt she owed him a bit of courtesy.

“You know what? I won’t pry. What are the details?” she asked, un-clipping her pocket field terminal[footnote:fr6dv] from her winter coat.

%Footnote.fr6dv: _Spravka-M_ (Справка-М [IPA_ru:ˈsprafkə ɛm]): An encrypted, pocket-sized tactical inquiry terminal issued to Soviet law enforcement officers. Encased in ruggedized, glove-friendly polycarbonate with a high-contrast e-paper display, it interfaces directly with district civil registries and local precinct databases via secure burst radio packets.

“I have a name: Anatoley Viktorovich Ruzhin,” he said slowly, doing his best to recall the exact characters from his vision.

She repeated the name aloud to confirm it before nodding. “Right. Let me check.” Unlocking the terminal, she triggered its voice command. “Dezhurny[footnote:fjhf9], look up a citizen in this district named Anatoly—” The moment she pronounced the given name, Luka cut in.

%Footnote.fjhf9: _Dezhurny_ (Дежурный [IPA_ru:dʲɪˈʐurnɨj]): Literally “Duty Officer” (derived from French _de jour_). On the _Spravka-M_ terminal, it refers to the lightweight, quantized offline artificial intelligence assistant that parses natural voice and text queries into structured municipal registry searches.

“It’s Anatoley with an ‘ey’,” he clarified.

Nadya glanced at him and chuckled. “With an ‘ey’? Pfft. That’s so… twenty-first century.”

Luka shrugged. That was simply what had been carved into the stone. Parents gave their children idiosyncratic spelling variations to make them stand out all the time.

Nadya engaged the voice command once more, this time articulating the precise spelling. In the blink of an eye, the AI assistant refreshed the display with the query results. Nadya, however, was wholly unprepared for what appeared.

On the high-contrast e-paper screen, the database returned a single entry. Exactly one match, corresponding letter-for-letter to the name Luka had just provided. Was it sheer coincidence? Or was he playing some elaborate prank on her?

“…I assume you don’t know this person, right?” she asked, looking for any rational explanation to make sense of it.

He shook his head earnestly. Luka’s own face tightened as he registered her reaction; he hadn’t anticipated such an immediate hit either.

“T-Then how did you—”

“The vision,” Luka broke in before she could finish. “I looked at the corpse, and I saw a cluster of headstones… Anatoley died today. Exactly today,” he explained, his voice weighed down with exhaustion.

A chill ran down Nadya’s spine, making her visibly stiffen. Her fingers tightened around the casing of the terminal, her lips trembling slightly. The look in Luka’s eyes was the exact same raw, haunted expression he had worn when describing Alex’s future crime earlier that morning. In that single moment, every shred of doubt evaporated—not out of sentimental trust, but under the sheer, undeniable weight of empirical fact.

“E-Excuse me… do you mind waiting right here? I—I need to report this to my superior,” she stammered, gripped by sudden panic. Luka gave a quiet nod of acknowledgment, and she turned on her heel, hurrying back toward the cordoned perimeter.

Nadya was not the only one who felt the air grow impossibly heavy. When Luka had first resolved to report Alex to the Militsiya that morning, he had felt one foot out the door of sanity—yet deep down, he had still clung to a desperate sliver of hope. A hope that these visions were merely some bizarre neurological symptom, an illusion born of stress and exhaustion.

Perhaps he had worked alongside Alex for too long and started projecting paranoid fantasies. Perhaps his peripheral vision had subconsciously caught the brass plaque on Nadya’s counter, allowing him to guess her name. Up until now, those prior occurrences could still be rationalized away as hallucinations or coincidences.

This time, however, rationalization was impossible. There was no conceivable way he could have known the full tripartite name of a total stranger, letter-for-letter down to the patronymic. The bizarre spelling of Anatoley’s name only sealed the verdict. This was, beyond all shadow of a doubt, a **premonition**.

Under ordinary circumstances, for a theoretical physicist, an empirical confirmation of this magnitude would be cause for celebration. His theoretical model was vindicated. Yet that vindication stripped away his last line of psychological defense: it meant Alex’s future crime was just as real, looming closer by the hour, and he had no idea how to stop it.

With both feet now out of the door, the freezing wind of truth had never felt so cold.

Luka found a snowbank-cleared bench nearby and sat down, staring blankly into the crowd for several long minutes as he tried to process what this meant.

Before long, the bystanders parted to make way for a squad of officers, with Nadya trailing closely behind. Leading them was a tall, stoic man with an austere, weathered countenance. The moment Nadya gestured in Luka’s direction, the group altered their course, heading straight toward him.

Luka could sense the distinct scent of trouble in the air, but retreat was out of the question. All he could do was brace himself for the inevitable confrontation.

The stoic officer stopped before him. “You are Luka Huo, correct?”

Luka rose slowly to meet his gaze. “Yes, Comrade Officer.”

The officer gave a clipped nod, offering a formal, gloved hand. “Citizen Huo. I am Senior Investigator, Major Tarasov. Viktor Danilovich.”

Luka took the firm, rigid handshake. Tarasov continued without breaking eye contact,

“Officer Morozova informed me that you provided a critical piece of information regarding this investigation. She briefed me on all the particulars, so I am here to ask you a single question.” His gaze bore down on Luka with piercing intensity.

“Are you certain you have never met Citizen Ruzhin prior to today?”

It was the very same question Nadya had asked him right before the realization settled in. Glancing at Nadya to his left, Luka saw her face drawn in a knot of anxiety and hesitation. At this stage, there was no playing it off as a joke. He had only one answer to give.

“Yes, I am certain, Comrade Major.”

Tarasov looked aside, nodding slowly to himself for a moment before snapping his gaze back onto Luka. “In that case, Citizen Huo, I am afraid you have just made yourself our prime suspect. Please accompany us to the station.”

The directive was crisp, calm, and absolute. Before Luka could even formulate an objection, Nadya burst in, “C-Comrade Major! Comrade Huo is our witness, not a suspect! His vision revealed—”

Tarasov wheeled on her with sharp, authoritative exasperation. “Morozova! I realize you are young and eager, but do I truly have to spell this out for you?” He cut her off with a pointed gesture toward Luka. “Look at the facts. This man used you to gain direct access to an active crime scene!”

Nadya froze. The thought had never once crossed her mind. She looked between Tarasov and Luka, rapidly replaying the entire morning in her head. Horrifyingly, from a procedural standpoint, it made complete sense. If Luka were indeed the perpetrator or an accomplice, he had effortlessly come close to the scene right under her wing without raising an inkling of suspicion.

Caught in a whirlwind of confusion and doubt, Nadya lowered her gaze. “Understood, Comrade Major…”

Tarasov turned his attention back to Luka. “Now, I do not know what manner of game you are attempting to play with these so-called _visions_ of yours. But let me remind you: this is the Militsiya, not a convention of occultists. We deal in material evidence, not _premonitions_.” He threw another dry, reproving glance at Nadya.

In Tarasov’s eyes, accepting such a premise was absurd—and Luka could hardly blame him. Even Luka himself had refused to believe it until five minutes ago.

“…Am I under arrest, Comrade Major?” Luka asked quietly, knowing the answer would determine his immediate future.

“No,” Tarasov replied flatly. “We do not yet possess formal probable cause. You may consider yourself fortunate that we do not take your _visions_ as a fact—at least not yet.”

He paused, then added in a measured tone, “However, I possess more than sufficient reasonable suspicion to open an official inquiry into your person and issue a municipal travel ban.[footnote:fhgc1]”

%Footnote.fhgc1: _Podpiska o nevyezde_ (Подписка о невыезде [IPA_ru:pɐtˈpʲiskə ɐ nʲɪˈvɨjɪzʲdʲɪ]): An official written recognizance of non-departure issued by an investigator under the Soviet Code of Criminal Procedure. Serving as a preventive restriction, it legally bars a suspect or key person of interest from leaving the municipal district boundaries during an active investigation.

Hearing the word ‘no’ was enough to make some of the tension drain from Nadya’s shoulders.

“Now, since our initial work at the scene is concluded, you will ride with us back to the precinct,” Tarasov gestured toward a waiting dark-blue patrol car.

Luka had no viable option but to comply. Flanked by officers on either side, he was escorted across the snow.

The sight inevitably drew the eyes of onlookers and neighbors, sparking immediate murmurs. To anyone standing on the perimeter, it bore all the hallmarks of an arrest.

Taking note of the police escort and the sudden stir in the crowd, Lin Chen caught sight of Luka being led away. Alarmed by what she saw, she broke through the perimeter line and hurried over.

“Little Huo, what happened?! Why are you being—” she called out in rapid Dongbei Chinese, hoping to slip past the officers’ comprehension.

Tarasov cut in without missing a beat. “Please do not obstruct officers in the execution of their duties, Correspondent!”

Luka looked back at her with weary eyes, offering a small, reassuring nod so she wouldn't make the situation worse. Lin stopped in her tracks, watching with furrowed brows as Luka was ushered into the back of the patrol car before the vehicle pulled away into the snow…










